  "method": "To evaluate a firm's net-working capital position, we need to consider the conflict between liquidity and profitability objectives. The current ratio (Current Assets / Current Liabilities) and the quick ratio (Quick Assets / Current Liabilities) are used to assess liquidity, while the return on assets (Net Income / Total Assets) and return on equity (Net Income / Total Equity) are used to assess profitability. Each formula is weighted by the specific components of the balance sheet and income statement it divides by.",
   "calculations": [
       {"label": "Current Ratio", "expression": "(1000000 / 500000)"},
       {"label": "Quick Ratio", "expression": "(700000 / 500000)"},
       {"label": "Return on Assets", "expression": "(200000 / 3000000)"},
       {"label": "Return on Equity", "expression": "(200000 / 1000000)"}
   ],
   "keyPoints": [
       "Liquidity refers to a firm's ability to meet its short-term obligations",
       "Profitability refers to a firm's ability to generate earnings",
       "The current ratio and quick ratio are used to assess liquidity",
       "The return on assets and return on equity are used to assess profitability",
       "A trade-off exists between liquidity and profitability, as increasing liquidity may decrease profitability and vice versa"
